---
import Subpage from "../layouts/Subpage.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";
---

<Subpage
  title="Privacy: nothing you type leaves your computer"
  description="Nothing you type leaves your computer. What unflub shares, with no ID attached, and how to turn it off: a daily update check, usage counts and crash reports."
  crumb="Privacy"
>
  <h1>Nothing you type leaves your computer.</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    You choose everything unflub does. No hidden settings. The code is public and free for anyone to read. Here is exactly what is
    shared.
  </p>

  <h2>What you type</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Corrections are worked out on your computer. Your text is never sent anywhere.</li>
    <li>
      unflub holds the word you are typing, and the one before it for context, in memory. It never writes what you type to disk.
    </li>
    <li>The words you misspell, and what they were fixed to, are never collected.</li>
    <li>
      Password fields are always skipped. Terminals and password managers are skipped by default, and you can add any other app.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The daily update check</h2>
  <p>Once a day, unflub asks whether a newer version is out. The request carries this and nothing else:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the unflub version, such as {SITE.version}</li>
    <li>your Windows version and processor type, x64 or ARM</li>
    <li>three yes or no flags: whether this is the first check today, this week and this month</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The flags let us count how many people use unflub each day, week and month. There is no ID, so nothing ties one check to
    the next or to you. Turn off Automatic updates on the About page in Settings and unflub stops checking.
  </p>

  <h2>Usage stats and crash reports</h2>
  <p>
    On first run, unflub asks whether to share these. They are on unless you say no, and one switch in Settings turns them off
    any time.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Usage stats.</strong> Anonymous counts, such as corrections per day, how often a fix gets undone, which settings are
      on and how long a check takes. Never a word you typed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Crash reports.</strong> If unflub crashes, it sends where in the code it failed. Any text is scrubbed out first.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your Stats page stays home</h2>
  <p>
    The Stats page in the app shows your corrections this week, the words you get wrong most and how many keystrokes unflub
    saved you. It is worked out and kept on your computer, and none of it is sent.
  </p>

  <h2>No account, no ads</h2>
  <p>There is no account and no advertising ID. We don't sell or share data, and unflub shows no ads.</p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    The site is hosted on Vercel and doesn't set cookies. We count visits with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which uses no cookies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Downloads are verified by Cloudflare Turnstile. When you point at or tab to a download button, your browser loads
    Turnstile from Cloudflare, and clicking runs a quick check before the download starts. What Cloudflare collects for that
    check is covered by its <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/">Turnstile Privacy Addendum</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you arrived from a link with a source on it, such as <span class="path">?ref=hn</span>, this tab remembers that source
    until you close it, so the download is counted under it.
  </p>

  <h2>Your settings</h2>
  <p>
    Your settings, your words and your shortcuts are saved in a file on your computer. Uninstalling unflub removes the app and its
    login entry, and the uninstaller asks whether to delete that file too.
  </p>

  <h2>Check for yourself</h2>
  <p>
    The code is public, so you do not have to take our word for any of this. <a href={SITE.repo}>Read it on GitHub</a>, or
    <a href={SITE.issues}>open an issue</a> if you have a question.
  </p>

  <p class="small updated">Last updated November 2026. unflub is an <a href={SITE.org.url}>RMGN</a> product.</p>
</Subpage>

<style>
  .updated {
    margin-top: 48px;
  }
</style>
